<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00V5">Definition <span data-tag="00V5">3.2.1.5</span>.</a> A <em>pointed simplicial set</em> is a pair $(X,x)$, where $X$ is a simplicial set and $x$ is a vertex of $X$. If $X$ is a Kan complex, then we refer to the pair $(X,x)$ as a <em>pointed Kan complex</em>. If $(X,x)$ and $(Y,y)$ are pointed Kan complexes, then a <em>pointed map</em> from $(X,x)$ to $(Y,y)$ is a morphism of Kan complexes $f: X \rightarrow Y$ satisfying $f(x) = y$. We let $\operatorname{Kan}_{\ast }$ denote the category whose objects are pointed Kan complexes and whose morphisms are pointed maps. </p>
